## Optimize costs without mistaking search progress for proof

### What you will learn

Read optimization results and distinguish display filtering from projection.

### Choose the cheapest candidate

The objective sums the selected fare. Search may report improving models before proving a minimum. optN asks for optimal-model enumeration; select snapshots whose proof flag is true rather than assuming the first model is optimal.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    fare(bus,3). fare(tram,1). fare(taxi,7).
    1 { take(Mode) : fare(Mode,Cost) } 1.
    #minimize { Cost,Mode : take(Mode), fare(Mode,Cost) }.
    #show take/1.
    """,
    models: :all,
    arguments: ["--opt-mode=optN"]
  )

true = result.exhausted
proven = Enum.filter(result.models, & &1.optimality_proven)
true = proven != []
true = Enum.all?(proven, &(&1.shown == ["take(tram)"] and &1.cost == [1]))
Course.display(result)
```

### Showing less does not merge models

Two hidden choices produce four models even when every shown set is empty. Projection onto the shown atoms identifies them as one projected answer. Use projection when your application defines equivalence through a visible signature.

```elixir
source = "{hidden(1..2)}. #show."
plain = Clingo.solve!(source, models: :all)
projected = Clingo.solve!(source, models: :all, arguments: ["--project=show"])
4 = length(plain.models)
1 = length(projected.models)
true = Enum.all?(plain.models, &(&1.shown == []))
%{ordinary_models: length(plain.models), projected_models: length(projected.models)}
```

### Try it yourself

Give bus the same fare as tram. Which proven optimal shown answers should remain?

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Set fare(bus,1). The optimal cost is [1]; proven snapshots include take(bus) and take(tram). Compare sets and proof flags rather than the order of search snapshots.

</details>
